2025-12-24 15:06:39 | INFO | Q996 id=BV1624y1u7Fj_0-00_0-30 | baseline_pred=B | gold=D | prompt_variant=medium | followup_pred=B | followup_correct=False | raw=Based on the provided information, it seems that there is a triplet with a total duration of one beat occurring around the 17th second. The metronome rhythm indicates that each beat should be divided into four quarter notes, so we can count the number of beats until we reach a total duration of one beat. \n\nAt the 15-second mark, we have 3 beats left (since 60 / 4 = 15), which is not enough for a triplet with a total duration of one beat. \n\nAt the 22-second mark, we have 2 beats left (since 60 / 4 = 15, and 22 - 15 = 7 > 1), which is enough for a triplet with a total duration of one beat.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B) around the 22nd second.
